/-!
# Descent of sections of a set-valued étale sheaf along a finite surjection

Let `f : X ⟶ Y` be a finite surjective morphism of schemes and let `𝒢` be a sheaf of sets on the
small étale site of `Y`.  The first two levels of the Čech nerve of `f` are `X₀ = X` with
`f₀ = f`, and `X₁ = X ×_Y X` with `f₁ = pr₀ ≫ f = pr₁ ≫ f`.  This file proves that global
sections on `Y` are the equalizer of the two canonical maps on global sections of the inverse
images,

`Γ(Y, 𝒢) = Equalizer (Γ(X₀, f₀⁻¹𝒢) ⇉ Γ(X₁, f₁⁻¹𝒢))`,

which is [Stacks, Tag 09Z3].

Injectivity of the fork map is separatedness of `𝒢` for the covering of the final object of the
small étale site of `Y` generated by `f`:  two global sections whose pullbacks to `X` agree agree
locally on `Y`, since `f` is surjective.  Surjectivity is the descent statement, and is where
finiteness enters.  A global section `s` of `f⁻¹𝒢` whose two pullbacks to `X₁` agree is, by local
description of the inverse image, locally on `X` the pullback of a section `a` of `𝒢` over an
object `U` of the small étale site of `Y`.  The locus in `U ×_Y X` on which the pullback of `a`
agrees with `s` is open, and the descent hypothesis says exactly that it contains the whole fibre
of `U ×_Y X ⟶ U` over each of its points;  because `f` is finite, the projection `U ×_Y X ⟶ U` is
closed, so the complement of that locus has closed image and may be removed from `U`.  After this
shrinking `a` already maps to `s`, so the sections `a` glue:  their images under the unit
`𝒢 ⟶ f_*f⁻¹𝒢` agree because that unit is injective, and `𝒢` is a sheaf.

The equalizer presentation is then used for Gabber's comparison [Stacks, Tag 09ZF]:  for a
closed immersion `i : Z ⟶ X` of a quasi-compact quasi-separated scheme such that restriction of
global sections of Zariski sheaves of sets along `i`, and along its base change by every finite
morphism, is bijective, restriction of global sections along `i` is bijective for every sheaf of
sets on the small étale site of `X`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.etaleGlobalSections` — global sections of sheaves of sets on the small
  étale site.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.etaleGlobalPullback` — pullback of a global section of a sheaf of sets
  on the small étale site along a morphism of schemes.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.etaleBaseChangeCompIso`,
  `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.etalePushforwardCompIso`,
  `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.etalePullbackCompIso` — the composition isomorphisms for base change,
  direct image and inverse image along a composite presented by an equation `p = g ≫ f`.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.relativeGlobalSectionsMap` — the induced map
  `Γ(Y, f⁻¹F) ⟶ Γ(Z, p⁻¹F)` for `p = g ≫ f`, which supplies the two parallel arrows of the
  equalizer.

## Main results

* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.globalSectionsPullback_isEqualizer_of_isFinite_surjective` — the fork
  map is injective, and its image consists of the sections with equal pullbacks to `X ×_Y X`:
  [Stacks, Tag 09Z3].
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.etaleGlobalPullback_eq_globalSectionsComparison_app` — the fork map is
  the component of `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.globalSectionsSmallEtaleInverseImageComparison`.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.isIso_globalSectionsSmallEtaleInverseImageComparison_of_zariskiBijective`
  — Gabber's comparison:  [Stacks, Tag 09ZF].

## Implementation notes

The source writes an equality of two sets.  It is stated here as bijectivity of the canonical
comparison `Γ(Y, 𝒢) ⟶ Γ(X₀, f₀⁻¹𝒢)` of
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.globalSectionsSmallEtaleInverseImageComparison` onto the subtype of
sections with equal pullbacks to `X₁`, which is what "is the equalizer" means for a canonical map
rather than for an arbitrary bijection.

`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.etaleBaseChangeCompIso` and
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.etalePushforwardCompIso` repeat
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.smallEtaleBaseChangeComp` and
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.smallEtalePushforwardCompIso` with the composite presented by an equation
`hp : p = g ≫ f` instead of literally as `g ≫ f`.  The generality is needed: the two arrows of
the equalizer are indexed by the two factorizations `pr₀ ≫ f` and `pr₁ ≫ f` of one and the same
morphism `f₁`, and a formulation that fixed the composite syntactically could not state that they
have the same target.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09Z3](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09Z3)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09Z2](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09Z2)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09ZF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09ZF)
-/

